---
import ProductLayout from '../../../layouts/ProductLayout.astro';
const title = 'Lower molar canal anatomy: find the missing distal canal';
const description = 'An illustrated guide to lower molar canal position, missed distal canals, chamber-floor landmarks, middle mesial canals, isthmuses and C-shaped anatomy.';
const schema = {'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'BlogPosting',headline:title,description,datePublished:'2026-10-26',dateModified:'2026-10-26',image:'https://endotechnz.com/blog/lower-molar/chamber-map.svg?v=canal-labels-20261026',author:{'@type':'Organization',name:'EndoTech NZ'},publisher:{'@type':'Organization',name:'EndoTech NZ'},isAccessibleForFree:true,articleSection:'Anatomy'};
const points = [
  ['Check both distal corners','A distal opening away from the centre should prompt a careful search for its partner.'],
  ['Read the chamber floor','Use the CEJ, floor–wall junction, colour change and developmental grooves together.'],
  ['Confirm the canal pathways','One opening can divide; two can merge. A filled canal does not prove that fins were cleaned.'],
  ['Refine access with restraint','Remove obstructing coronal dentine under vision; preserve the chamber floor and furcal wall.'],
  ['Assess the cause of the lesion','A root-specific lesion is a clue. It cannot, on its own, establish a missed canal or exclude fracture.'],
];
const checks = [
  ['Before access','Record symptoms, previous treatment and review interval; assess restorability, coronal seal, cracks, six-point probing and baseline periapical images.'],
  ['Orient the tooth','Identify mesial, distal, buccal and lingual from the tooth and images. Use the CEJ and root axis when a crown or restoration distorts the occlusal landmarks.'],
  ['Expose the chamber floor','Isolate with rubber dam; clear chamber contents and obstructing roof sufficiently to see the floor–wall junction. Do not flatten or gouge the floor.'],
  ['Audit the distal root','Inspect the full buccolingual extent of the distal opening. If it is eccentric, assess the opposite end and any connecting groove before accepting one canal.'],
  ['Audit the mesial root','Confirm MB and ML; inspect the groove between them for a middle mesial entry or fin. Treat a groove as anatomy to assess, not proof of a third canal.'],
  ['Confirm the pathways','Negotiate gently with small precurved hand files. Establish working length for each pathway; use angled views when files overlap and consider confluence.'],
  ['Disinfect the spaces','Maintain irrigant exchange, replenishment and safe backflow; use activation when appropriate. Mechanical enlargement cannot reach every recess.'],
  ['Stop and reassess','When landmarks disappear, dentine thickness is uncertain or negotiation is unsafe, stop cutting. Reassess imaging, consider indicated CBCT, or refer.'],
];
---
<ProductLayout title={`${title} | EndoTech NZ`} description={description} image="/blog/lower-molar/chamber-map.svg?v=canal-labels-20261026" schema={schema} accent="slate">
  <article class="molar-article">
    <header class="molar-hero">
      <a class="back" href="/resources/clinical-tips/">← Clinical education</a>
      <p class="eyebrow">Clinical anatomy · 26 October 2026 · 12 min read</p>
      <h1>Lower molar<br />canal anatomy.</h1>
      <p class="standfirst">A root filling can look satisfactory while part of the canal system remains untreated. The position and shape of a distal opening can help you find a second canal, a broad extension or a connecting isthmus.</p>
      <figure class="hero-map"><a class="desktop-diagram" href="/blog/lower-molar/chamber-map.svg?v=canal-labels-20261026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open this anatomy diagram at full size"><img src="/blog/lower-molar/chamber-map.svg?v=canal-labels-20261026" width="1200" height="660" alt="Occlusal chamber maps comparing three canals, four canals, and an eccentric distal entry with a possible missed distolingual canal." fetchpriority="high" /></a><div class="mobile-diagrams" role="group" aria-label="Swipe to compare anatomy diagrams"><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/chamber-map-panel-1.svg?v=canal-labels-20261026" width="400" height="570" alt="Three canals: MB, ML and one distal canal" loading="lazy" /><span>1 / 3 · Three canals: MB, ML and one distal canal</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/chamber-map-panel-2.svg?v=canal-labels-20261026" width="400" height="570" alt="Four canals: MB, ML, DB and DL" loading="lazy" /><span>2 / 3 · Four canals: MB, ML, DB and DL</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/chamber-map-panel-3.svg?v=canal-labels-20261026" width="400" height="570" alt="An eccentric distal entry and a possible second entry" loading="lazy" /><span>3 / 3 · An eccentric distal entry and a possible second entry</span></div></div><figcaption>Swipe the diagrams on a phone to compare larger panels. On desktop, click a diagram to open it at full size. Original teaching schematics. Orientation is fixed for comparison: mesial left, distal right, buccal above and lingual below. Canal positions vary between teeth.</figcaption></figure>
    </header>

    <aside class="key-points" aria-labelledby="key-title"><p class="eyebrow">Five clinical decisions</p><h2 id="key-title">Check the distal root before accepting there is only one canal.</h2><ol>{points.map(([heading,copy])=><li><strong>{heading}</strong><span>{copy}</span></li>)}</ol></aside>
    <nav class="article-jumps" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#case">The case</a><a href="#positions">Canal positions</a><a href="#floor">Floor landmarks</a><a href="#hidden-spaces">Fins &amp; isthmuses</a><a href="#variants">Variants</a><a href="#workflow">Chairside checks</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></nav>

    <section id="case" class="section split">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">01 / The source case</p><h2>A distal lesion after root canal treatment.</h2><p>The linked reel from Endo simplified / إندو ببساطة presents a previously treated lower molar with a distal periapical radiolucency and a comparatively unremarkable mesial root. The presenter considers disinfection and fracture, then reports finding a second distal canal during retreatment.</p><p>The explanation moves from rectangular and triangular chamber-floor patterns to long-neck Munce burs, isthmuses and the recesses of broad distal canals. Examine the full distal anatomy before accepting the canal count.</p><p>This is the presenter’s case account. The reel does not provide a complete diagnostic record or documented long-term healing.</p><a class="button" href="https://www.facebook.com/reel/1472058068141603/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch the original reel ↗</a><p class="caption">Source: Endo simplified. The original video remains on Facebook; access may require sign-in.</p></div>
      <aside class="diagnosis"><h3>Differential diagnosis</h3><dl><dt>Untreated anatomy</dt><dd>A second distal canal or an uncleaned extension may retain infection.</dd><dt>Persistent or recurrent infection</dt><dd>Review treatment quality, isolation, coronal leakage and the previous lesion’s healing trajectory.</dd><dt>Crack or vertical root fracture</dt><dd>Assess the clinical findings together. Neither normal probing nor absence of a J-shaped lesion rules it out.</dd></dl><p>Disease around only one root does not establish its cause, and an apparently healthy mesial root does not prove that isolation or disinfection was adequate. <a href="#ref-fracture">[7]</a></p></aside>
    </section>

    <section id="positions" class="section"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Canal positions</p><h2>Three or four canal openings.</h2><div class="two-column"><div><h3>Mesial: MB and ML</h3><p>The mesiobuccal and mesiolingual entries generally occupy the buccal and lingual ends of the mesial floor–wall junction. A developmental groove may connect them. Below the floor, the canals can remain separate, communicate through isthmuses, or merge. Root count, opening count and apical exit count describe different things.</p><h3>Distal: one broad entry, or DB and DL</h3><p>A single distal opening may be oval or elongated buccolingually. If you suspect there could be two canals, look for distal buccal and distal lingual openings along the distal wall-distal floor wall junction. An opening at one end of that junction should prompt inspection of the other end; it does not prove that another canal exists. <a href="#ref-krasner">[1]</a></p></div><div><h3>Triangular and quadrilateral patterns</h3><p>The common three-entry arrangement forms a triangle; four entries often give a quadrilateral. The connecting grooves may resemble a Y or H. Those letters describe the chamber-floor pattern. The canals can divide or merge below it.</p><p>A single distal opening need not sit exactly on the mesiodistal centreline. In Gorduysus and colleagues’ study of 50 extracted mandibular second molars, distal entries tended to lie lingual to the occlusal centre. Three openings occurred in 72%, two in 16%, four in 10% and one in 2%. The percentages apply to those 50 extracted teeth. Locate canals from the individual tooth’s landmarks. <a href="#ref-gorduysus">[2]</a></p></div></div>
      <figure><a class="desktop-diagram" href="/blog/lower-molar/distal-pathways.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open this anatomy diagram at full size"><img src="/blog/lower-molar/distal-pathways.svg" width="1200" height="660" alt="Longitudinal schematics of one distal canal, two distal canals merging into one, and two distal canals remaining separate." loading="lazy" /></a><div class="mobile-diagrams" role="group" aria-label="Swipe to compare anatomy diagrams"><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/distal-pathways-panel-1.svg" width="395" height="520" alt="One distal pathway" loading="lazy" /><span>1 / 3 · One distal pathway</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/distal-pathways-panel-2.svg" width="395" height="520" alt="Two distal pathways merging into one" loading="lazy" /><span>2 / 3 · Two distal pathways merging into one</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/distal-pathways-panel-3.svg" width="395" height="520" alt="Two separate distal pathways" loading="lazy" /><span>3 / 3 · Two separate distal pathways</span></div></div><figcaption>Coronal entries do not predict the number of apical exits. The routes are simplified examples; splitting, rejoining and lateral anatomy can add further complexity.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>

    <section id="floor" class="section tinted"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Chamber-floor landmarks</p><h2>Locate the CEJ and the floor–wall junction.</h2><p>Krasner and Rankow examined 500 extracted teeth and described relationships that make access more systematic. Apply these observations together and recognise when calcification, previous cutting or unusual anatomy has changed the map. <a href="#ref-krasner">[1]</a></p><div class="landmarks"><div><b>01</b><h3>CEJ and root axis</h3><p>The cementoenamel junction is a more consistent reference than the outline of a heavily restored crown. Estimate chamber position and depth before cutting.</p></div><div><b>02</b><h3>Colour and junction</h3><p>The floor is generally darker than the chamber walls. Inspect its full junction with the walls, particularly the angles and the ends of developmental grooves.</p></div><div><b>03</b><h3>Symmetry and its limits</h3><p>Use buccolingual pairing around a mesiodistal reference as a search aid in ordinary lower molars. Expect variation in spacing and symmetry, especially in C-shaped systems.</p></div></div><p class="clinical-note">Use the tooth’s landmarks to control cutting. Published inter-orifice distances vary with the tooth, population and measurement method. Preserve the floor’s landmarks and reassess whenever the expected anatomy is not present.</p></section>

    <section id="hidden-spaces" class="section"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Oval canals, fins and isthmuses</p><h2>A distal canal can be broader than the preparation inside it.</h2><p>An elongated opening can lead into an oval, ribbon-like or divided space. A round instrument may prepare the central pathway while leaving buccal and lingual extensions untouched. An isthmus is a narrow communication between canal spaces; a fin is a narrow extension from a canal. Neither is automatically a separately negotiable canal.</p><figure><a class="desktop-diagram" href="/blog/lower-molar/oval-and-isthmus.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open this anatomy diagram at full size"><img src="/blog/lower-molar/oval-and-isthmus.svg" width="1200" height="660" alt="A circular preparation leaves the ends of an oval canal untouched; a second cross-section shows an isthmus between two canals." loading="lazy" /></a><div class="mobile-diagrams" role="group" aria-label="Swipe to compare anatomy diagrams"><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/oval-and-isthmus-panel-1.svg" width="580" height="485" alt="Round preparation within an oval canal" loading="lazy" /><span>1 / 2 · Round preparation within an oval canal</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/oval-and-isthmus-panel-2.svg" width="580" height="485" alt="Isthmus connecting two canals" loading="lazy" /><span>2 / 2 · Isthmus connecting two canals</span></div></div><figcaption>A dense-looking root filling is a two-dimensional image of material. It cannot demonstrate that the full pulp space was mechanically contacted or disinfected.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="two-column"><div><h3>Refining access with a long-neck bur</h3><p>Long-neck Munce burs can improve visibility during coronal access refinement. Work under magnification and follow the chamber landmarks. Avoid drilling through the floor or chasing an isthmus down the root.</p><p>Ultrasonic tips or other suitable access instruments may be chosen according to the anatomy and operator’s training. Follow the instrument’s instructions, maintain direct vision and preserve dentine toward the furcation. When the floor–wall junction or depth cannot be identified confidently, stop and reassess.</p></div><div><h3>Irrigation in broad canal spaces</h3><p>Expose obstructed entries sufficiently to identify and negotiate them. Then establish safe irrigant delivery, replenishment and exchange; add activation where appropriate, allowing backflow and avoiding binding. Enlarging the root until every fin is touched is not a defensible cleaning strategy.</p><p>Fan and colleagues found isthmuses in the apical 5 mm of 85% of mesial roots in their micro-CT sample of 126 extracted Chinese mandibular first and second molars. This was a mesial-root anatomical study; it is not a distal-canal prevalence figure or proof of clinical treatment success. <a href="#ref-fan">[4]</a></p></div></div>
      <details class="classification"><summary>Understanding the isthmus classifications shown in the reel</summary><p><strong>Fan et al. (2010)</strong> describe three-dimensional mesial-root connections as sheet, separate, mixed and cannular. <strong>Hsu and Kim (1997)</strong> use five cross-sectional types, from separate canals without a connection to a complete connecting corridor. <strong>Weller et al. (1995)</strong> distinguish complete from partial connections. These systems describe different views of anatomy and should not be treated as interchangeable labels. <a href="#ref-fan">[4]</a> <a href="#ref-hsu">[9]</a> <a href="#ref-weller">[10]</a></p></details>
    </section>

    <section id="variants" class="section"><p class="eyebrow">05 / Anatomical variations</p><h2>Middle mesial canals, extra roots and C-shaped systems.</h2><h3>Inspect between MB and ML.</h3><p>A middle mesial canal may arise within the mesial developmental groove. It can remain independent, merge with MB or ML, or be associated with a coronal fin. A dark line is not proof of a separate canal, and repeated deep troughing can sacrifice valuable dentine.</p><div class="evidence"><strong>48 / 258<span>molars with a middle mesial canal</span></strong><p>Versiani et al. found an overall frequency of 18.6% in extracted Brazilian and Turkish mandibular first molars, with different rates in the two groups (22.1% and 14.8%). Confluent anatomy predominated; only 3 of the 48 teeth with an MMC had the fully independent configuration. The mean minor diameter of independent MMC openings was 0.16 mm. These laboratory measurements do not establish clinical detection rates or NZ prevalence. They should not determine bur size. <a href="#ref-versiani">[3]</a></p></div>
      <figure><a class="desktop-diagram" href="/blog/lower-molar/middle-mesial.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open this anatomy diagram at full size"><img src="/blog/lower-molar/middle-mesial.svg" width="1200" height="660" alt="Middle mesial search zone between MB and ML, with independent, confluent and fin patterns illustrated." loading="lazy" /></a><div class="mobile-diagrams" role="group" aria-label="Swipe to compare anatomy diagrams"><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/middle-mesial-panel-1.svg" width="390" height="420" alt="Search zone between MB and ML" loading="lazy" /><span>1 / 4 · Search zone between MB and ML</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/middle-mesial-panel-2.svg" width="245" height="475" alt="Independent middle mesial canal" loading="lazy" /><span>2 / 4 · Independent middle mesial canal</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/middle-mesial-panel-3.svg" width="245" height="475" alt="Confluent middle mesial canal" loading="lazy" /><span>3 / 4 · Confluent middle mesial canal</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/middle-mesial-panel-4.svg" width="240" height="475" alt="Fin configuration" loading="lazy" /><span>4 / 4 · Fin configuration</span></div></div><figcaption>In the fin configuration, the openings communicate coronally while three canals can leave through separate foramina. A confluent middle mesial pathway joins another mesial canal. The drawings simplify the source descriptions. [3]</figcaption></figure>
      <h3>An extra distal entry may belong to an extra root.</h3><p>A separate distolingual root is termed <em>radix entomolaris</em>; a supernumerary buccal root is termed <em>radix paramolaris</em>. Look for an additional root outline, unexpected curvature or a canal that does not match the visible root’s position. Establish the root configuration before assuming that every distal opening lies in one broad root. <a href="#ref-radix">[11]</a></p>
      <h3>Check lower second molars for a C-shaped system.</h3><p>Fused roots, a ribbon-like or semicolon-shaped opening, or seemingly disconnected entries may belong to a C-shaped system. Its cross-section can change from continuous to interrupted or separate spaces along the root. A round coronal opening does not exclude that arrangement below. Min and colleagues’ micro-CT study of 44 selected C-shaped second molars showed why the usual floor patterns and symmetry rules may not apply. This selected sample cannot estimate C-shaped prevalence in all lower molars. <a href="#ref-cshape">[5]</a></p>
      <figure><a class="desktop-diagram" href="/blog/lower-molar/c-shaped.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open this anatomy diagram at full size"><img src="/blog/lower-molar/c-shaped.svg" width="1200" height="660" alt="Conceptual cross-sections show a continuous C-shaped lumen, an interrupted C, and separate canal spaces." loading="lazy" /></a><div class="mobile-diagrams" role="group" aria-label="Swipe to compare anatomy diagrams"><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/c-shaped-panel-1.svg" width="400" height="480" alt="Continuous C-shaped space" loading="lazy" /><span>1 / 3 · Continuous C-shaped space</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/c-shaped-panel-2.svg" width="400" height="480" alt="Interrupted C-shaped space" loading="lazy" /><span>2 / 3 · Interrupted C-shaped space</span></div><div><img src="/blog/lower-molar/c-shaped-panel-3.svg" width="400" height="480" alt="Separate spaces in a C-shaped system" loading="lazy" /><span>3 / 3 · Separate spaces in a C-shaped system</span></div></div><figcaption>Recognise the system before enlarging it. Thin walls and connecting webs make indiscriminate dentine removal particularly hazardous.</figcaption></figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section tinted"><p class="eyebrow">06 / Micro-CT images and videos</p><h2>Marco Versiani’s Root Canal Anatomy Project</h2><p>Professor Marco A. Versiani’s <em>Root Canal Anatomy Project</em> uses micro-CT reconstructions to make canal systems visible from multiple directions. Use the original images and videos to look beyond the access opening: follow canal divisions, confluences, isthmuses, lateral branches and the relationship between internal and external anatomy.</p><div class="resource-links"><a href="https://rootcanalanatomy.blogspot.com/2011/03/mandibular-first-molar.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Mandibular first molar ↗</strong><span>Root Canal Anatomy Project · Marco Versiani</span></a><a href="https://rootcanalanatomy.blogspot.com/2018/08/mandibular-second-molar_20.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Mandibular second molar ↗</strong><span>Compare internal pathways with the root form.</span></a></div><p class="caption">Micro-CT is an ex vivo research method; routine clinical CBCT does not reproduce its fine anatomical detail. EndoTech drew the diagrams on this page to explain the anatomy. Follow the links above to see the Project’s original micro-CT images.</p></section>

    <section id="workflow" class="section"><p class="eyebrow">07 / Chairside checks</p><h2>From assessment to canal negotiation.</h2><ol class="workflow">{checks.map(([heading,copy])=><li><strong>{heading}</strong><p>{copy}</p></li>)}</ol><p>Use additional angled periapical views when superimposition obscures the canal pattern. Consider limited-field CBCT when the examination and conventional images leave a specific question unanswered and the result could change management. Select an appropriate field and exposure, and interpret the entire volume; CBCT is not a routine screening step or a reliable way to exclude every fine crack. <a href="#ref-cbct">[8]</a></p><p class="clinical-note">Stop cutting when the anatomy is unclear. Reassess the images and chamber landmarks, or refer before removing more dentine.</p></section>

    <section id="sources" class="section sources"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence &amp; original sources</p><h2>References</h2><p>The Facebook reel supplies the case discussion. Anatomical studies support the search principles and their limits. Laboratory findings do not establish a patient’s canal configuration or the outcome of a treatment.</p><ol>
      <li id="ref-krasner"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1097/00004770-200401000-00002" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Krasner P, Rankow HJ. Anatomy of the Pulp-Chamber Floor. J Endod. 2004;30:5–16.</a> Chamber landmarks and orifice-location principles.</li>
      <li id="ref-gorduysus"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2591.2009.01596.x" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gorduysus O et al. Localization of root canal orifices in mandibular second molars in relation to occlusal dimension. Int Endod J. 2009;42:973–977.</a> Fifty extracted second molars; opening position and variation.</li>
      <li id="ref-versiani"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26556548/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Versiani MA et al. Middle mesial canals in mandibular first molars: a micro-CT study in different populations. Arch Oral Biol. 2016;61:130–137.</a> DOI: 10.1016/j.archoralbio.2015.10.020. Published online in 2015; journal issue 2016.</li>
      <li id="ref-fan"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20951302/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fan B et al. Three-dimensional morphologic analysis of isthmuses in the mesial roots of mandibular molars. J Endod. 2010;36:1866–1869.</a> Four three-dimensional isthmus patterns in a selected extracted-tooth sample.</li>
      <li id="ref-cshape"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joen.2006.07.004" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Min Y et al. C-shaped Canal System in Mandibular Second Molars. Part III: The Morphology of the Pulp Chamber Floor. J Endod. 2006;32:1155–1159.</a> Chamber-floor variations in 44 selected C-shaped molars.</li>
      <li id="ref-project"><a href="https://rootcanalanatomy.blogspot.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Versiani MA. The Root Canal Anatomy Project.</a> Original micro-CT teaching images and videos; first- and second-molar links above.</li>
      <li id="ref-fracture"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39840523/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Patel S et al. Position statement on longitudinal cracks and fractures of teeth. Int Endod J. 2025;58:379–390.</a> ESE consensus supporting a combined clinical and imaging assessment.</li>
      <li id="ref-cbct"><a href="https://newsroom.aae.org/press-releases/aae-and-aaomr-release-joint-position-statement-on-the-use-of-cbct-in-endodontics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AAE / AAOMR. Use of Cone-Beam Computed Tomography in Endodontics: 2025 Update.</a> Current imaging position statement announcement and linked guidance.</li>
      <li id="ref-hsu"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9248689/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hsu YY, Kim S. The resected root surface. The issue of canal isthmuses. Dent Clin North Am. 1997;41:529–540.</a> Cross-sectional isthmus classification.</li>
      <li id="ref-weller"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7499980/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Weller RN, Niemczyk SP, Kim S. Incidence and position of the canal isthmus. Part 1. Mesiobuccal root of the maxillary first molar. J Endod. 1995;21:380–383.</a> Complete and partial connections; this study concerns upper molars, not lower-molar prevalence.</li>
      <li id="ref-radix"><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joen.2006.05.007" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Calberson FL et al. The radix entomolaris and paramolaris: clinical approach in endodontics. J Endod. 2007;33:58–63.</a> Additional-root recognition and treatment implications.</li>
    </ol><p class="caption">Case source: <a href="https://www.facebook.com/share/v/1CKC5C7JGp/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Endo simplified’s original Facebook post</a>. For dental professionals. Apply the findings to the individual tooth, diagnosis, imaging and operator’s training.</p></section>
